From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: bind the Type-C controller (TUSB320)

The single USB-C port has no Type-C driver, so role selection is done by
hand: the rhodep-usb-otg package pokes the SGM41542 charger's OTG_CONFIG bit
over i2c and flips the dwc3 role switch from a script. It works but is manual
and does not react to the cable.

The port controller is an SGM7220, which is a register-level clone of the TI
TUSB320, and mainline already drives that with extcon-usbc-tusb320. Confirmed
on the device before adding the node: on the charger i2c bus (bus 3 here),
address 0x47 answers, registers 0x00-0x07 read back the ASCII string "TUSB320"
and register 0x09 reports a live attached-state with a cable-orientation bit.

Add the "ti,tusb320" node at 0x47 with its IRQ on tlmm 11 (vendor sgm7220.dtsi
intr_gpio), and a usb-c-connector whose OF-graph endpoint links to the dwc3
role switch. The dwc3 node was already prepared for this (dr_mode = "otg",
usb-role-switch, role-switch-default-mode = "peripheral"); this adds the
matching port endpoint so the connector resolves to it. With this the
controller reports orientation and CC role and sets host/device on the dwc3
automatically, which is what the manual OTG script was standing in for.

The default role stays "peripheral" so the USB gadget (and SSH over it) still
comes up on a plain cable; host mode engages when a host cable/OTG adapter is
detected.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts	2026-09-23 16:08:14.941395005 +0000
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts	2026-09-23 16:08:37.227592002 +0000
@@ -691,6 +691,39 @@
 		monitored-battery = <&battery>;
 		input-current-limit-microamp = <1000000>;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * SGM7220 Type-C port controller. Confirmed on the device to be a
+	 * register-level clone of the TI TUSB320: i2c bus 3 (this bus), address
+	 * 0x47, registers 0x00-0x07 read back the ASCII id "TUSB320", and
+	 * register 0x09 reports a live attached-state/orientation. Mainline
+	 * drives it with extcon-usbc-tusb320 (compatible "ti,tusb320").
+	 *
+	 * It reports cable orientation and the CC role, and through the
+	 * usb-c-connector graph below it flips the dwc3 role switch
+	 * automatically, replacing the manual OTG_CONFIG poke in
+	 * packages/rhodep-usb-otg. IRQ is tlmm 11 (vendor sgm7220.dtsi
+	 * intr_gpio = <&tlmm 11 0x0>), active-low.
+	 */
+	typec@47 {
+		compatible = "ti,tusb320";
+		reg = <0x47>;
+		interrupts-extended = <&tlmm 11 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING>;
+
+		connector {
+			compatible = "usb-c-connector";
+			label = "USB-C";
+			data-role = "dual";
+			power-role = "dual";
+			try-role = "sink";
+
+			port {
+				tusb320_hs_ep: endpoint {
+					remote-endpoint = <&dwc3_hs_ep>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+	};
 };
 
 &i2c10 {
@@ -1193,6 +1226,18 @@
 	 */
 	usb-role-switch;
 	role-switch-default-mode = "peripheral";
+
+	/*
+	 * Graph link back to the Type-C connector on the SGM7220/TUSB320. The
+	 * connector's fwnode_usb_role_switch_get() resolves through this port to
+	 * this dwc3 role switch, so the Type-C controller can set host/device
+	 * automatically on cable orientation and CC role.
+	 */
+	port {
+		dwc3_hs_ep: endpoint {
+			remote-endpoint = <&tusb320_hs_ep>;
+		};
+	};
 };
 
 &usb_1_hsphy {
